  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nZonal flow aloft continues across the Northern Plains as the surface \nlow over northern Minnesota move eastward. Satellite imagery shows \nclouds continuing to clear across the west as temperatures warm into \nthe 70s. There is a chance that some of the low clouds hang on \na little longer in central and eastern North Dakota then what \nmodels have forecast. Tonight, overnight lows are forecast to \ndrop down around the mid 40s out west as surface high pressure \nmoves into the region with 50s elsewhere. Sunday, high \ntemperatures are forecast to warm into the 70s and 80s across \nthe area as surface high pressure will clear skies before a \nweak ridge moves across the High Plains. There is some marginal\ninstability possible across the southwest tomorrow afternoon \nwhich could result in a low chance of strong storms. The CSU ML \nprogram is pegging a low chance for severe weather tomorrow\nafternoon. An upper level wave could kick storms off in the \nafternoon and evening continuing precipitation into the over \nnight hours mainly across the southern half of the state. \nOvernight low Sunday are forecast to drop down into the 50s.\n\nMonday, temperatures will continue to warm into the 80s as the \nridge out west strengthens. There is another low chance of \nstrong to severe storms in the southeastern half of the state \nextending into the James River Valley Monday afternoon. This \nwill bring a front to the region which will slightly cool \ntemperatures Tuesday afternoon. Another wave could move through \nthe region Tuesday bringing forth low to medium chances of rain \nacross the state. Low chances for precipitation will continue on\nthrough the week tapering off by the weekend as an upper level \nridge builds over the region which will lead to warmer weather. \nThis ridge could push temperatures back into the upper 80s to \nlower 90s next weekend.",
